   "l": "Under heaven nothing is softer and weaker than water,\nyet for attacking the hard-and-strong\nnone it can overcome;\nas for it — there is no means by which to exchange it.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Nothing under heaven is softer or weaker than water, and yet for attacking what is hard and strong nothing can better it — there is nothing that could take its place.",
   "n": "莫 … 於 is the comparative you met at chapter 46 (禍莫大於不知足, 'no calamity greater than not knowing when you have enough') and again at 69 (禍莫大於輕敵): 莫 is a negative with its subject already inside it, 'nothing', and 於 after the bare adjective supplies the 'than' that Chinese has no comparative ending for.\n\nNow look hard at 莫之能勝. The object pronoun 之 stands in front of the verb 勝, not after it. This is a rule, not an accident: in a negative clause Classical Chinese pulls a pronoun object forward, ahead of the verb. So the four characters are 'none | it | can | overcome'. It is one of the few places where word order does the work that an ending would do in a European language, and once you have seen it you will see it everywhere.\n\n其無以易之: Wang Bi says plainly that 以 here is 用, 'to use', and that 其 is the water — use water's softness and weakness, and there is nothing that can be put in its place. 易 is the 'exchange' sense of the character, not the 'easy' sense; both live in the same graph.",
   "p": true
  },
  {
   "t": "弱之勝強，\n柔之勝剛，\n天下莫不知莫能行。\n是以聖人云﹕「\n受國之垢，\n是謂社稷主；\n受國不祥，\n是為天下王。」\n正言若反。",
