   "l": "The weak's overcoming the strong,\nthe supple's overcoming the rigid —\nunder heaven none does not know it, none can walk it.\nTherefore the sage says:\n'Receive the state's filth —\nthis one is called lord of the altars of soil and grain;\nreceive the state's ill omen —\nthis one is king of all under heaven.'\nStraight words seem the reverse.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "That the weak overcome the strong and the supple overcome the rigid — there is no one under heaven who does not know it, and no one who can do it. So the sage says: 'Take the state's filth upon yourself and you are called lord of the altars; take the state's ill fortune upon yourself and you are king of all under heaven.' Words that go straight sound like their own reverse.",
   "n": "弱之勝強 is chapter 75's grammar again: 之 set between a subject and its verb makes the whole clause into a noun, so the line is not a statement but a thing — 'the-weak-overcoming-the-strong' — which the next line then says everybody knows.\n\n莫不知莫能行 puts two 莫 side by side, each carrying its own subject. 莫不 is a double negative that lands as an emphatic positive: there is nobody who does not know it. And then, without a connective of any kind, the second 莫 takes it all back.\n\n社稷 are two altars: 社 the mound where the god of the soil was served, 稷 where the god of the grain was. Keeping their sacrifices was the first duty of a ruler, so the pair became the everyday name for the state itself — 'the altars' standing for the realm much as English says 'the crown'. To lose one's 社稷 was to lose the country.\n\n垢 is dirt ground in, the grime that will not wash off, and by extension disgrace. 不祥 is not simply bad luck: it is what the diviners called ill-omened, the thing nobody will touch. Both are what a ruler is supposed to shed onto someone else.\n\nThe two halves of the quotation vary their verb — 是謂 'is called' in the first, 是為 'is' in the second — where English would repeat. Classical parallel couplets prefer the variation. In both, 是 is the demonstrative 'this one'; the language still has no verb 'to be', and 為 in the second is doing that work as an ordinary verb, 'counts as'.\n\nHere is one of the book's surviving rhymes, and you can hear it. 強 qiáng, 剛 gāng, 祥 xiáng and 王 wáng all belong to one Old Chinese rhyme group, and Mandarin has kept the chime in every one of them — the couplet rhymes and the quotation closes on the same sound. 行 xíng was in that group too; that one Mandarin has lost.\n\n正言若反 is the chapter's name for the whole book, and 反 is not merely 'the opposite'. It is the same character as chapter 40's 反者道之動 — 'turning back is the Way's movement'. Straight speech sounds like a reversal because reversal is the direction the Way actually runs.\n\nThe quotation marks are an editor's. The original has no punctuation at all, and where the sage's words stop — before 正言若反, or including it — is a judgement, not a fact."
